    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammaburg">Hammaburg</a>, a fortification built in the early ninth century near the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alster">Alster</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, probably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, to secure the Frankish frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Around 832 it became the seat of a missionary archbishopric under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a>, the apostle of the North, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sacked the settlement in 845 and the see moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. Over the following centuries the small cathedral town was repeatedly destroyed by Slavic and Danish raids. Its revival came under the Schauenburg counts of Holstein: in about 1188 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-iii-of-holstein">Adolf III</a> granted a group of merchants land to found a new town (<em>Neustadt</em>) beside the old episcopal settlement, with a harbour on the Alster. A charter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> dated 7 May 1189, granting the citizens freedom from tolls on the lower Elbe and other rights, became the basis of the city's later privileges. Historians have shown it to be a forgery made in the mid-thirteenth century, but the date is still celebrated each year as the port's birthday. The old and new towns merged in 1216. In 1241 Hamburg concluded an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> to protect the road between the two cities, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic, an agreement often seen as one of the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hamburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamburg.jpg" alt="Hamburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamburg,_Landungsbr%C3%BCcken_--_2016_--_3131-7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dietmar Rabich, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> lived from trade and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, which connected it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> upstream and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> downstream. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the North Sea gateway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, where Baltic goods brought overland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> were shipped onwards, and it was above all a brewing city: in the fourteenth century several hundred breweries exported beer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. In the sixteenth century the city admitted newcomers who brought new trades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Flemish refugees, Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> and, from 1567, the English Merchant Adventurers made Hamburg a centre of the cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and Iberian trades. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-stock-exchange">Hamburg Exchange</a> was founded in 1558 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburger-bank">Hamburger Bank</a> in 1619. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was introduced in 1529 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a>'s church order, and the city remained strictly Lutheran; Reformed Protestants, Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> long had limited rights, and many worshipped in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altona">Danish Altona</a>. In the eighteenth century Hamburg prospered from trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Atlantic world. In the nineteenth it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s greatest port: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-america-line">HAPAG</a>, founded in 1847, grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ballin">Albert Ballin</a> into the largest shipping company in the world by 1914, and millions of emigrants left through the port. Rapid growth brought crowded housing and an unfiltered water supply, exposed by the cholera epidemic of 1892, which killed some 8,600 people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/u-boat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/u-boat.jpg" alt="U-boat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U995_2001_1_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darkone; derivative work by Georgfotoart, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> was never a military power and survived mainly through walls, diplomacy and neutrality. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its ships fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>; in 1401 a Hamburg fleet captured leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victual-brothers">Victual Brothers</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaus-stortebeker">Klaus Störtebeker</a> and his men were executed in the city. Hamburg also acquired positions along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> to protect shipping, holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritzebuttel">Ritzebüttel</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuxhaven">Cuxhaven</a> at the river mouth from 1394. Its greatest threat came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, whose kings, as rulers of Holstein after 1460, claimed Hamburg as a Holstein town; a Danish siege in 1686 failed. Between 1616 and 1625 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-van-valckenburgh">Johan van Valckenburgh</a> surrounded the city with modern bastioned fortifications, and behind them Hamburg stayed neutral and unoccupied throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War, profiting from the trade of the warring powers. That neutrality ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. French troops occupied the city in 1806, and in 1813–1814 Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Louis-Nicolas Davout</a> held it against a Russian-led siege, expelling thousands of poorer inhabitants in midwinter. In the twentieth century Hamburg's shipyards, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blohm-voss">Blohm & Voss</a>, built warships such as the battleship <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-battleship">Bismarck</a></em> and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boats</a>. The Allied raids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-gomorrah">Operation Gomorrah</a> in July and August 1943 created a firestorm that killed around 35,000 people, and the city surrendered to British forces without a fight on 3 May 1945.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court.jpg" alt="Imperial Chamber Court" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Audienz_Reichskammergericht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> was ruled by a council (<em>Rat</em>) of merchants and lawyers, which co-opted its own members and chose burgomasters from among them, while the authority of the counts of Holstein faded during the thirteenth century. The city's law was written down in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordeelbook">Ordeelbook</a></em> of 1270, one of the earliest German city law codes. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-recess">Long Recess</a> (<em>Langer Rezess</em>) of 1529 gave the propertied citizens, organised by parish, a share in government alongside the council, a balance confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/main-recess">Main Recess</a> of 1712, which declared council and citizenry jointly sovereign. Hamburg's status was long disputed: Emperor Maximilian I summoned it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diet</a> as a free imperial city in 1510, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a> confirmed its immediacy in 1618, but the Danish kings accepted it only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gottorp">Gottorp Settlement</a> of 1768. The city held a small rural territory, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vierlande">Vierlande</a>, which it governed jointly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>. After 1815 Hamburg was one of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> of the German Confederation. The constitution of 1860 created a modern system with a Senate as executive and an elected Bürgerschaft, although the franchise favoured property owners until 1919. Since 1952 a democratic constitution has governed the Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg as a federal state, with a First Mayor heading the Senate and the Bürgerschaft as its parliament.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/speicherstadt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/speicherstadt.jpg" alt="Speicherstadt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/speicherstadt">Speicherstadt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Speicherstadt_abends.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>'s influence has been economic and cultural. As one of the leading Hanseatic cities it helped knit together the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic, and its alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> formed the backbone of that network. In the early modern period its openness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English, Portuguese and other merchants made it a model commercial republic, and its exchange, bank and marine insurers anchored northern European commerce. Its cultural life was unusually rich for a merchant city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oper-am-gansemarkt">Oper am Gänsemarkt</a>, opened in 1678, was the first public opera house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-keiser">Reinhard Keiser</a> and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">George Frideric Handel</a> worked; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-philipp-telemann">Georg Philipp Telemann</a> was the city's director of music from 1721 to 1767, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-philipp-emanuel-bach">Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a> wrote his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-dramaturgy">Hamburg Dramaturgy</a></em> for the city's short-lived National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in 1767–1769, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> were born in Hamburg. In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries the port and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-america-line">HAPAG</a> tied Germany to world markets, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/speicherstadt">Speicherstadt</a> warehouse district, built from 1883, and the Kontorhaus district with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilehaus">Chilehaus</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2015. After 1945 Hamburg became the centre of the West German press, home to <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-spiegel">Der Spiegel</a></em>, <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/die-zeit">Die Zeit</a></em> and <em>Stern</em>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-schmidt">Helmut Schmidt</a>, who directed relief work during the North Sea flood of 1962, rose from Hamburg politics to become federal chancellor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>'s independence was interrupted rather than ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the start of 1811 as chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bouches-de-l-elbe">Bouches-de-l'Elbe</a>, and the city regained its freedom only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Davout</a>'s garrison left in May 1814. As a free city of the German Confederation it recovered quickly, even after the Great Fire of May 1842 destroyed about a quarter of the old town, including the town hall. Hamburg joined the North German Confederation in 1867 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871 as a federal state, entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> in 1888 on condition of keeping a free port. After the revolution of 1918 the Bürgerschaft was elected by universal suffrage, and the city weathered a failed communist uprising in October 1923. Under the Nazi regime its self-government was subordinated to a Reich governor, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act">Greater Hamburg Act</a> of 1937 joined it with the Prussian towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altona">Altona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wandsbek">Wandsbek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harburg-wilhelmsburg">Harburg-Wilhelmsburg</a> while transferring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuxhaven">Cuxhaven</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. After the devastation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the British military government re-established Hamburg as a state, with elections in October 1946, and since 1949 it has been a state of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. With around 1.9 million inhabitants it is the country's second-largest city and largest port, one of the busiest container ports in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and a centre of shipping, aircraft manufacturing and media.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> rose from a Carolingian fort and a twelfth-century merchant town to become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> gateway, a free imperial city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s greatest port, and it survives as a self-governing city-state of the Federal Republic, a rare continuity of urban republican government since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</em></p>

